//! The routines that call nothing.
//!
//! 3227 of them: read by the same walk as [`crate::nested_routines`], recorded in
//! the same shape, and every one of them reaches the end without calling a
//! single recovered function. They ask questions, they loop, they work
//! something out, and they do it all themselves.
//!
//! That is a claim about what could be seen, so it is qualified. 137 of them do
//! call through a thunk the decompiler could not resolve — there is no
//! recovered function behind it, but there is a call — and those carry
//! [`Routine::through_a_thunk`]. For the other 3090 nothing was called at all.
//!
//! They branch more freely than routines that call things. Of every step in
//! this table, about **23 in a hundred** is a branch reached because the
//! questions before it failed; in the call-making table next door it is about
//! 17 in a hundred. That difference is what these routines are: where a routine
//! that calls things works through a sequence, one of these chooses between
//! answers, and choosing is what an `else` is for.
//!
//! 1309 of them are a single step — one question, or one loop, and nothing else.
//! 159 of those single steps are a bare loop that calls nothing, which is a scan:
//! walking something to find a value or a length, with the whole of the work in
//! the loop's own condition.

// The addresses are written without separators so the port-mapping importer can
// match them against the recovered functions.
